From ecf720ae46983f80aa71954877d061559ea79f2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:07:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: an OOM-killed command takes the command, not
 the run (OOMPolicy=continue)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A Kanban run scope used systemd's default OOMPolicy=stop: when the kernel
killed one runaway tool command at the run's MemoryMax, systemd stopped the
whole scope, agent included, and the run was lost ("Worker was OOM-killed at
8192 MiB (peak 373.559 MiB)", run 36817; 29 such runs in 14 days).

- Kanban run scopes get `--property OOMPolicy=continue` when a one-time
  systemd-run probe accepts it (systemd >= 253; workers and hub run 255).
  Older systemd (239/245/249 reject it on transient scopes, #102486) keeps
  today's argv and logs once. The shared availability probe and generic
  scope argv still never carry OOMPolicy. MemoryMax and KillMode unchanged.
- A foreground terminal command that exits by SIGKILL while the run scope's
  memory.events oom_kill count rose now says "killed by the OOM killer at the
  run's memory cap (<MemoryMax>); run it under `systemd-run --user --scope -p
  MemoryMax=…` or reduce its footprint" (tools/terminal_oom.py).
- describe() never prints a peak below the limit for an oom-kill: it names
  the kernel's victim (journalctl -k: task, pid, anon-rss) when readable,
  else "peak unknown".
- Under continue, the worker's own oom_kill snapshot no longer marks its exit
  as an OOM failure; a run whose agent is itself the kernel's victim (the
  largest process) still fails as oom_killed, named "the worker itself".

Failure injection on worker-3 (real dispatcher argv, 512 MiB cap, a
foreground command allocating 1.5 GiB): old core -> worker rc -15,
"Worker was OOM-killed at 512 MiB"; this change -> command exit 137 with the
new note, card completed; agent-as-victim -> oom_killed, "kernel victim: the
worker itself".
